codex
Concrete LLVM execution now passes representative boundary, prime, and composite cases, including both prompt examples. The proof definition formalizes primality as a recursive “no divisor in `[2,n)`” function and uses the Python loop state itself as the induction measure, so the proof is universal in `n`, `x`, and `y` rather than a finite test enumeration.
diff --git a/concrete_tests.py b/concrete_tests.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..fce5751e242eaefa367345f81596f0bddca24933
--- /dev/null
+++ b/concrete_tests.py
@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+def x_or_y(n, x, y):
+    if n < 2:
+        return y
+    for divisor in range(2, n):
+        if n % divisor == 0:
+            return y
+    return x
+
+
+assert x_or_y(7, 34, 12) == 34
+assert x_or_y(15, 8, 5) == 5
+assert x_or_y(1, 7, 9) == 9
+assert x_or_y(2, 11, 13) == 11
+assert x_or_y(0, 4, 6) == 6
+assert x_or_y(97, -2, 10) == -2
+assert x_or_y(49, 3, 4) == 4
